law/persistence.sl.content:
shape law.persistence : law {
  type: law
  layer: 0
  """
// Law 0: A shape persists stably only if its structure is coherent.
// Incoherent shapes dissolve. This is the axiom.
//
// The engine implements this as: validate() checks all shapes,
// edit() propagates waves to maintain coherence after mutation,
// the store persists only what the engine holds.
//
// On a shape machine, this law IS the persistence mechanism.
// In emulation, engine.validate + engine.store implement it.
fn check_persistence(id) {
  if !exists(id) {
    absorb
  }
  let dp = deps(id)
  for dep in dp {
    if !exists(dep) {
      flag
    }
  }
  auto "persists"
}
"""
}

law/consistency.sl.content:
shape law.consistency : law {
  type: law
  layer: 0
  """
// Law 3: Coupled incompatible shapes must resolve or trigger dissolution.
// Contradictions in contact cannot persist. The engine resolves this
// through block (withdraw permission) and propagation (force resolution).
//
// engine.block implements this: when an author is blocked, existing
// shapes persist (Law 2) but get flagged with content warnings.
// The wave propagates the inconsistency until it resolves.
fn check_consistency(id) {
  let dt = dependents(id)
  for dep in dt {
    let w = dim(dep, "warning")
    if w != "" {
      flag
    }
  }
  auto "consistent"
}
"""
}

law/conservation.sl.content:
shape law.conservation : law {
  type: law
  layer: 0
  """
// Law 2: No structure from nothing, no destruction into nothing.
// You cannot remove a shape that others depend on.
// You cannot create a shape without the system recording it.
// Every mutation advances the tick. Every change is traced.
//
// engine.trace implements conservation of history.
// rm checks dependents before allowing removal.
fn check_conservation(id) {
  if !exists(id) {
    flag
  }
  auto "conserved"
}
"""
}

law/reference.sl.content:
shape law.reference : law {
  type: law
  layer: 0
  """
// Law 1: References must close, point to invariants, or be bounded.
// Dangling references are incoherent. The engine tracks all deps
// and validates reference closure.
//
// engine.propagate implements this: when a shape changes, the wave
// follows references to maintain closure. Dependents must respond.
fn check_reference(id) {
  let dp = deps(id)
  for dep in dp {
    if !exists(dep) {
      flag
    }
  }
  auto "references close"
}
"""
}

theory/safe-reference.sl.content:
shape theory.safe-reference : theory.minimal {
  type: theorem
  layer: 0
  """
// Theorem 4.5 (Safe Reference).
// empty_s never changes (Theorem 4.2). Therefore any shape whose
// transformation depends on empty_s will never encounter a change
// in what empty_s presents: empty_s returns the same character at
// every moment. A reference to empty_s can never produce a surprise.
//
// Derives from: theory.minimal (fixed point property)
  """
}

theory/transformation.sl.content:
shape theory.transformation : theory.moment {
  type: theorem
  layer: 0
  """
// Theorem 3.4 (Transformation).
// Persistence requires transformation: M -> M'.
//
// By the axiom, persistence requires the capacity to change.
// This capacity requires a transformation mechanism: a prior moment M
// and an emergent moment M', where M' is what M becomes.
// M' = M may hold (no change occurs), but the transformation
// M -> M' must be defined.
//
// Derives from: theory.moment (transformation operates on moments)
  """
}

theory/discreteness.sl.content:
shape theory.discreteness : theory.transformation {
  type: theorem
  layer: 0
  """
// Theorem 3.5 (Moment Discreteness).
// The moment sequence is inherently discrete. Each moment M' is
// produced by exactly one application of the transformation law.
// There is no fractional application.
//
// The transformation takes complete input (C, S) and produces
// complete output (M'). Any proposed intermediate state would itself
// be a moment, introducing additional steps, not subdividing the step.
//
// Every shape has a structural minimum: the simplest coherent
// configuration. Below this minimum, the structure cannot sustain
// coherent transformation. The moment sequence has a structural floor.
//
// Note: discreteness of the moment sequence does not imply discreteness
// of character values. Character can take values in a continuous space.
// The continuous limit is well-defined but unreachable.
//
// Derives from: theory.transformation
  """
}

theory/constants.sl.content:
shape theory.constants : theory.mixing.angle, theory.coupling.conservation-stacking, theory.dimension.geometry {
  type: theorem
  layer: 0
  """
// Section 13 (Structural Constants).
// The three geometries together determine measurable quantities
// for any shape system. This section derives constants for the
// 3D lattice (N=3): the simplest system with nontrivial internal
// space. Each constant follows from the counting procedure with
// no free parameters. The choice of N selects which structure to
// analyze; the derivations are forced given N=3.
//
// Definition 13.1 (Shape Constant): sigma(d) = (d-1)/d.
// The maximal source fraction. The remaining 1/d is the
// moment-sequence dimension: the structural ground on which
// transformation operates.
//
// Theorem 13.2 (Minimal Source): c=1, d=2 -> p=3, sin(theta)=1/3.
// The simplest persistent shape with distinguishing character.
//
// 3D lattice: 3 spatial + 6 internal + 1 moment-sequence = d=10.
//
// 13.4.1 Phase-Chiral: sin^2(theta) = 3/13 ~ 0.2308
// 13.4.2 Oscillation: sin^2(theta) = 4/13 ~ 0.3077
// 13.4.3 Isolated Internal: sin(theta) = 2/9 ~ 0.2222
// ... and more constants from coupling configurations.
//
// Constants are structural measurements, not free parameters.
// If you know the structure, you can derive the constants.
// If you measure the constants, you can identify the structure.
//
// Derives from: theory.mixing.angle, theory.coupling.conservation-stacking,
//               theory.dimension.geometry
  """
}

theory/coupling.sl.content:
shape theory.coupling : theory.contact.mutual, theory.coherence.law1 {
  type: definition
  layer: 0
  """
// Section 10 (Coupling).
// The relational geometry of coupled shapes. Law 1 (Stable Reference)
// is the primary driver: stable reference between coupled shapes
// requires joint coherence, producing conservation stacking at
// shape junctions.
//
// Definition 10.1 (Dynamic Coupling): shapes A and B are dynamically
// coupled when changes to one affect the transformation of the other
// through mutual contact. Coupling admits degrees: direct contact
// creates strong coupling, contact through intermediaries creates
// attenuated coupling.
//
// Derives from: theory.contact.mutual, theory.coherence.law1
  """
}

theory/algebra.sl.content:
shape theory.algebra : theory.emergence.general-composition, theory.minimal, theory.landscape {
  type: definition
  layer: 0
  """
// Section 16 (The Algebra of Shapes).
//
// 16.1 Structural Equivalence (Def 16.1): A ~= B when a
// correspondence preserves transformation and reference.
// Reflexive, symmetric, transitive.
//
// 16.2 Identity Element (Thm 16.3): under identity structure,
// empty_s is the identity for composition: A compose empty_s ~= A.
//
// 16.3 Continuity as Structure (Thm 16.4): continuity across
// transformation IS structure S. Two moments belong to the same
// shape iff they share the same structure.
// Corollary 16.4a (Identity): a shape's identity is its complete
// trace: structure + character together.
//
// 16.4 Structural Change (Def 16.5): structural continuity
// requires a chain of intermediates where each differs in at most
// one structural feature. Structural distance is the minimum
// chain length (Def 16.7).
//
// 16.5 Mixing angle of structural change (Thm 16.8).
//
// 16.6 Dual Perspective (Thm 16.11): individual vs system
// perspective. Neither reducible to the other.
//
// 16.7 Cross-Perspective Coherence (Thm 16.12): the two
// perspectives must be mutually coherent.
//
// Derives from: theory.emergence.general-composition,
//               theory.minimal, theory.landscape
  """
}

theory/coherence.sl.content:
shape theory.coherence : theory.contact.self {
  type: definition
  layer: 0
  """
// Definition 5.6 (Coherence).
// A structure S is coherent when it can produce well-defined
// transformation at every moment. This requires three conditions:
//   (1) all contacts through which character enters transformation
//       are stable
//   (2) transformation conserves structure
//   (3) coupled incompatible shapes resolve
//
// Laws 1-3 derive why each condition is necessary.
// Theorem 5.9 proves they are jointly sufficient.
//
// Derives from: theory.contact.self
//               (coherence is derivable from self-contact alone)
  """
}

theory/fractal.sl.content:
shape theory.fractal : theory.coherence, theory.coherence.contact-is-shape, theory.emergence {
  type: theorem
  layer: 0
  """
// Theorem 7.1 (Fractal Coherence).
// The Laws of Coherence apply identically at every level of
// emergence. An emergent shape is a shape, and its persistence
// requires the same coherence conditions as any other shape.
// There is no structural distinction between "fundamental" and
// "emergent."
//
// Let D be emergent from A and B. D is a shape. All shapes must
// satisfy Laws 0-3. The contact between A and B is itself a shape
// and must satisfy Laws 0-3 independently. By induction on emergence
// layers, the laws apply at every level.
//
// This is not a separate requirement imposed on emergent shapes.
// It is a consequence of the fact that emergent shapes ARE shapes,
// and contact IS a shape. The recursion grounds at the axiom.
//
// Derives from: theory.coherence (laws apply to all shapes),
//               theory.coherence.contact-is-shape (contact is a shape),
//               theory.emergence (emergence produces shapes)
  """
}

theory/moment.sl.content:
shape theory.moment : theory.structure, theory.character {
  type: definition
  layer: 0
  """
// Definition 3.3 (Moment).
// A moment M is a distinguishable state of a shape: a specific
// character C under a specific structure S.
//
// Derives from: theory.structure, theory.character
// (a moment is the combination of both aspects)
  """
}

theory/transformation-law.sl.content:
shape theory.transformation-law : theory.prior-reference, theory.structure {
  type: theorem
  layer: 0
  """
// Theorem 3.9 (Transformation Law).
// The transformation from moment to moment has the form:
//
//   M' = f(C, S)
//
// where:
//   C is the prior moment's character (what the configuration was)
//   S is the shape's structure (the geometry governing transformation)
//   M' is the emergent moment (what the shape becomes)
//   f is the transformation function: the mapping from (C, S) to M'
//
// The theorem excludes:
//   (a) M' cannot depend on anything other than C and S
//   (b) f is deterministic (same inputs, same output)
//   (c) C must be the prior state's character (not arbitrary input)
//
// f is single-valued: same character and structure always produce the
// same emergent moment. If the same inputs could produce different
// outputs, continuity would fail.
//
// Derives from: theory.prior-reference (C must enter f),
//               theory.structure (S governs transformation)
  """
}

theory/uniqueness.sl.content:
shape theory.uniqueness : theory.minimal {
  type: theorem
  layer: 0
  """
// Theorem 4.6 (Uniqueness).
// There is exactly one minimal shape. Not one "up to structural
// equivalence," but one.
//
// Suppose two minimal shapes exist. Both have no distinguishing
// character beyond self-reference. Both have identical structure.
// To be two distinct shapes, something must distinguish them.
// But both have identical structure (minimal), identical character
// (none), and identical reference (self only). In persistence
// structure, identity is structural: two things are the same thing
// if and only if they have the same structure and the same character
// trajectory. Therefore they are identical.
//
// Derives from: theory.minimal
  """
}

theory/fixed-point.sl.content:
shape theory.fixed-point : theory.distinguishing-character, theory.self-reference {
  type: theorem
  layer: 0
  """
// Theorem 4.2 (Fixed Point of the Minimal Shape).
// A shape with no distinguishing character beyond self-reference is
// a fixed point of its own transformation: M' = M.
//
// Let B be such a shape. Its character references only its own prior
// character, and that character has no content beyond the reference
// itself. Suppose M' includes content X that M lacks. Then B has
// character beyond self-reference, contradicting the premise. Suppose
// X is transient: then B has the dynamical pattern of generating and
// removing X, which is itself distinguishing character. Therefore no
// new content can appear, even transiently. M' = M.
//
// Derives from: theory.distinguishing-character,
//               theory.self-reference
  """
}

theory/prior-reference.sl.content:
shape theory.prior-reference : theory.continuity, theory.structure, theory.character {
  type: theorem
  layer: 0
  """
// Theorem 3.7 (Mandatory Prior-State Reference).
// The transformation producing M' must be determined in part by
// the prior moment's character.
//
// Continuity requires that M' relate to M as "the same thing in a
// new state." Structure S is shared by all moments of the same shape
// (it is constant). Therefore S alone cannot specify which prior
// moment M' continues from. The specific state is the character C.
// Without dependence on prior character, M' would be determined by
// S alone, making it the same regardless of which prior state obtained.
//
// Derives from: theory.continuity (requires connection to specific M),
//               theory.structure (S alone is insufficient),
//               theory.character (C distinguishes specific states)
  """
}

theory/mixing.sl.content:
shape theory.mixing : theory.transformation-law, theory.contact, theory.coherence.law2 {
  type: theorem
  layer: 0
  """
// Section 9 (The Self-Transformation).
// The internal geometry of a single transformation M -> M'.
// Law 2 (Conservation) is the primary driver.
//
// The transformation reads character through contact. A shape's
// context determines which contacts contribute. Structural analysis
// requires a scope: a boundary around the specific transformation
// being measured.
//
// Derives from: theory.transformation-law (the form M'=f(C,S)),
//               theory.contact (character enters through contact),
//               theory.coherence.law2 (conservation forces geometry)
  """
}

theory/distinguishing-character.sl.content:
