//
// The periodic table is the canopy of atomic structure: every
// coherent electron configuration that persists around every
// coherent nucleus count. The structure (quantum numbers, shell
// filling rules, exclusion) generates the table. The 118 elements
// are the traces through this structure.
//
// Derives from: theory.physics.particles (electron + baryon spectrum),
//               theory.physics.chirality (mass, charge, spin),
//               theory.physics.hadrons (nuclear structure)
  """
}

theory/axiom.sl.content:
shape theory.axiom : theory {
  type: axiom
  layer: 0
  """
// Definition 2.1 (Persistence).
// Persistence is the capacity to change while maintaining continuity.
//
// Non-vacuity: the cogito. Denial instantiates it. The act of
// denying persistence requires persisting through the denial.
// Exhibition, not syllogism.
//
// The definition contains no undefined primitives. "Change" and
// "continuity" are the two requirements, in tension: something must
// differ (change) while something connects states as states of the
// same thing (continuity).
//
// Existence is persistence. To exist is to persist: to be present
// across at least one transformation, maintaining continuity through
// it. A thing that does not persist does not exist.
//
// The axiom inherits the cogito's epistemic status. It cannot be
// coherently denied. It is definitional, not axiomatic: it applies
// to anything that matches the definition, and the definition cannot
// be coherently denied.
  """
}

theory/character.sl.content:
shape theory.character : theory.shape {
  type: definition
  layer: 0
  """
// Definition 3.2 (Character).
// The character of a shape, denoted C, is the aspect that changes:
// the specific configuration at a given point, the particular state
// right now.
//
// Character is what distinguishes one state from another state of
// the same shape. Two shapes with identical structure may differ in
// character: traversing different sequences of states.
//
// Structure corresponds to continuity. Character corresponds to change.
// The axiom requires both; the shape has both.
//
// Crucially, character is itself persistence structure. The values
// character takes (positions, angles, configurations) are themselves
// structural. Everything is persistence structure at every level of
// the fractal. The distinction between S and C is a distinction of
// role at a given level, not two ontological categories.
//
// Derives from: theory.shape (the axiom forces the S/C decomposition)
  """
}

theory/forcing.sl.content:
shape theory.forcing : theory.coupling.load-bearing, theory.coherence.law3, theory.coherence.states {
  type: definition
  layer: 0
  """
// Section 11 (Forcing, Collapse, and Resolution).
// The resolution geometry: what happens when coupled shapes are
// incompatible. Law 3 (Consistency) is the primary driver.
//
// Definition 11.1 (Forcing Shape): any shape F whose presence
// increases lambda(A,B), bringing incompatible A and B into more
// direct coupling by collapsing intermediary shapes.
//
// Theorem 11.2 (Decoherence Stability): decoherent context persists
// as long as contact between incompatible shapes remains mediated.
//
// Theorem 11.3 (Decoherence Collapse): when forcing increases lambda
// beyond what partial coherence can sustain, decoherence collapses.
//
// Theorem 11.4 (Anchored Forcing): forcing anchored to high-lambda
// shapes forces collapse because excluding the forcing shape would
// degrade coherence.
//
// Derives from: theory.coupling.load-bearing, theory.coherence.law3,
//               theory.coherence.states
  """
}

theory/structure.sl.content:
shape theory.structure : theory.shape {
  type: definition
  layer: 0
  """
// Definition 3.1 (Structure).
// The structure of a shape, denoted S, is the aspect that stays the
// same and governs the transformation: the geometry of how the shape
// persists. Structure defines what kind of shape this is.
//
// Forced by the axiom: persistence requires both change and continuity.
// By excluded middle, every aspect either changes or does not. What
// does not change is structure. What changes is character.
// Exactly two categories, exhaustive and mutually exclusive.
//
// Structure is not passively invariant. It must govern the transformation.
// If it did not, any change could produce any result, and there would
// be no continuity between one state and the next. The invariant aspect
// must determine how change happens. This is what makes it structure:
// it is load-bearing. It carries the transformation.
//
// Structure is constant across ordinary transformations. It can change
// through coherence resolution with other shapes (structural change,
// derived in theory.algebra.structural-change).
//
// Derives from: theory.shape (the axiom forces the S/C decomposition)
  """
}

theory/computing.sl.content:
shape theory.computing : theory.physics.lattice, theory.chemistry.properties, theory.transformation-law {
  type: system
  layer: 0
  """
// A Complete Computer from Persistence Structure.
//
// A computer is a shape that implements the transformation law
// M' = f(C, S) in silicon. The transformation law is universal;
// the computer makes it programmable: S is changeable (software),
// not fixed. This is the structural content of general-purpose
// computation.
//
// Architecture layers (emergence hierarchy):
//   0. Silicon: doped semiconductor, electron flow
//   1. Transistor: voltage-controlled switch (the minimal gate)
//   2. Logic gates: NOT, NAND, NOR, AND, OR, XOR
//   3. Arithmetic: adder, multiplexer, decoder, comparator, ALU
//   4. Sequential: flip-flop, register, counter (memory elements)
//   5. Functional: register file, memory array, cache line
//   6. Datapath: pipeline stages, buses, forwarding
//   7. Control: instruction decoder, control unit, hazard detection
//   8. Processor: CPU core, cache hierarchy, MMU
//   9. System: memory controller, I/O controller, interrupt, bus
//  10. Computer: power supply, clock, boot sequence, peripherals
//
// Each layer is emergent composition (Theorem 6.2): the composite
// has properties absent from its components. A flip-flop stores
// a bit; no individual gate stores anything. A pipeline executes
// instructions; no individual stage does.
//
// The idealized architecture: 64-bit RISC, 5-stage pipeline,
// 3-level cache, virtual memory, interrupt-driven I/O.
// Modern in capability, clean in structure.
//
// Derives from: theory.physics.lattice (spatial substrate),
//               theory.chemistry.properties (silicon chemistry),
//               theory.transformation-law (what computation IS)
  """
}

theory/continuity.sl.content:
shape theory.continuity : theory.transformation {
  type: theorem
  layer: 0
