//
// The three laws constrain geometry directly:
//   Law 1 (Stable Reference) requires balanced reference along
//     each generator, forcing bidirectional structure.
//   Law 2 (Conservation) requires both chiral handednesses to
//     exist with equal structural weight.
//   Law 3 (Consistency) requires contact between neighboring
//     shapes, determining the unique packing geometry.
//
// Derives from: theory.emergence (dimensionality requires shape systems),
//               all three relational laws (constrain geometry)
  """
}

theory/contact.sl.content:
shape theory.contact : theory.transformation-law {
  type: definition
  layer: 0
  """
// Definition 5.1 (Contact).
// Contact is the structural relationship through which character
// enters a transformation. Contact has structure (the geometry
// governing what enters and how) and character (the specific content
// carried at each moment). Contact is itself a shape: it persists
// across moments and satisfies the Laws of Coherence.
//
// Contact is the structure of reference. Reference is the character
// of contact: the specific content carried across the contact boundary
// into a transformation. Contact is the shape; reference is what the
// shape produces.
//
// Derives from: theory.transformation-law (contact is how C enters f)
  """
}

theory/category.sl.content:
shape theory.category : theory.algebra.emergent-existence, theory.transformation-law {
  type: theorem
  layer: 0
